power: supply: max17042: bind "maxim,max77705-battery" as an I2C client

The MAX77705 fuel gauge is a MAX17047-class gauge on its own I2C address
(0x36). Upstream only reaches it as a platform child of the max77705 MFD:
"max77705-battery" sits in max17042_platform_id and in the OF table, but
not in max17042_id. Described directly on the bus, the client is named
"max77705-battery", i2c_client_get_device_id() returns NULL, and
max17042_i2c_probe() falls through to the ACPI lookup and returns
-ENODEV, silently.

On the Galaxy Tab S8+ the MAX77705 top (0x66) is not described (its
revision, PASS2, is rejected by drivers/mfd/max77705.c), so the gauge is
described as a plain I2C client. Add the name to the I2C ID table with
the same chip type the platform table uses.

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
index d409d2f0d..f4433c60f 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
@@ -1373,6 +1373,7 @@ static const struct i2c_device_id max17042_id[] = {
 	{ .name = "max17050", .driver_data = MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX17050 },
 	{ .name = "max17055", .driver_data = MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX17055 },
 	{ .name = "max77759-fg", .driver_data = MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX77759 },
+	{ .name = "max77705-battery", .driver_data = MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX17047 },
 	{ .name = "max77849-battery", .driver_data = MAXIM_DEVICE_TYPE_MAX17047 },
 	{ }
 };
